/// Calculates the median of a mutable slice of u32 in $O(n)$ average time complexity.
pub fn calculate_median(slice: &mut [u32]) -> Option<f64> {
    let len = slice.len();
    if len == 0 {
        return None;
    }

    let mid = len / 2;
    if len % 2 == 1 {
        // Odd length: the median is the single middle element.
        let (_, &mut val, _) = slice.select_nth_unstable(mid);
        Some(val as f64)
    } else {
        // Even length: partition around the upper middle element.
        let (left, &mut val2, _) = slice.select_nth_unstable(mid);
        // The lower middle element must be the maximum value in the left partition.
        let val1 = *left.iter().max()?;
        Some((val1 as f64 + val2 as f64) / 2.0)
    }
}
```

### Why `select_nth_unstable` is Chosen

1. **Performance ($O(n)$ vs $O(n \log n)$)**: A full sort (using `sort` or `sort_unstable`) takes $O(n \log n)$ time. `select_nth_unstable` implements the introselect algorithm, which rearranges the slice such that the element at the target index is in its final sorted position, with smaller elements to its left and larger to its right. This achieves an average time complexity of $O(n)$.
2. **Memory ($O(1)$ Auxiliary)**: It operates entirely in-place. Sorting algorithms often require auxiliary memory (up to $O(n)$ for stable sorts), and copying the slice to a new `Vec` would require $O(n)$ memory allocation. Using `select_nth_unstable` avoids any heap allocations.

