An elegant mathematical property of the XOR operation simplifies this problem. For any partition of the array into three contiguous subarrays $X_1, X_2,$ and $X_3$, the XOR sum of these segments is:

$$X_1 \oplus X_2 \oplus X_3 = \left(\bigoplus_{r=0}^{i} A[r]\right) \oplus \left(\bigoplus_{r=i+1}^{j} A[r]\right) \oplus \left(\bigoplus_{r=j+1}^{N-1} A[r]\right) = \bigoplus_{r=0}^{N-1} A[r]$$

Because XOR is associative and commutative, and the three segments partition the array exactly, their XOR sum is invariant of the partition indices $i$ and $j$. It is always equal to the XOR sum of the entire array. Thus, the maximum value is simply the XOR sum of the whole array (provided a valid partition exists, which requires $N \ge 3$).

### C++ Implementation

